.\" Revision 1.12  2026/10/17 23:50:18  dlr
.\" procrule 1.36 accepts -u as well as -8 for the UTF-32 rule engine. The -8 entry now says so, and says which to use: -u was this option own spelling through 1.30 and is kept so a script written against that binary still runs, but -8 is the spelling to prefer because mdxfind(1) spells it -8 too, its own -u being a userid file, so one letter means one thing in both tools. Placed as a paragraph inside the -8 entry rather than as an entry of its own, since an alias with no behaviour of its own does not deserve a .TP and splitting it would invite the reader to treat the two as different options. SYNOPSIS is left showing -8 alone, deliberately: it is the form being recommended. Renders clean under groff -mandoc.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.11  2026/09/15 12:32:03  dlr
.\" -8 is the option that runs the UTF-32 engine, -A is documented for the first time, and the -L example was unusable as printed.
.\"
.\" THE FLAG. It is -8 in both procrule and mdxfind: mdxfind -u loads a userid file, so -8 is the only spelling that can mean the same thing in both tools. Every rendered occurrence on this page now names it. This was not cosmetic -- procrule has no -u at all, its optstring is "?hvVx8qZAL:r:R:t:p:M:m:o:l:s:B:G:D:N:S:H:C:", and anyone following the previous page got "procrule: invalid option -- u" on the primary route to the engine.
.\"
.\" -A, WHICH HAD NO ENTRY ANYWHERE. It emits one line per (word, rule) with nothing suppressed; where a line would be absent a marker takes its place, :SAME output equals input, :EMPTY nothing left, :REJECT a rejection rule fired, :BADRULE the rule could not be used, :NEG anything else. It forces $HEX[] on, because a candidate containing a colon is $HEX[]-wrapped and so no ordinary line can begin with one, which is what keeps the markers unambiguous. Match mode is unaffected. It is the counterpart to the DESCRIPTION statement that procrule never emits the source word -- the switch that makes that suppression visible and countable.
.\"
.\" The entry states that both engines report the same marker for the same outcome, which is the point of the option and is not free: the two engines number their internal results INVERSELY, a rejection being -1 to one and -3 to the other, and the UTF-32 engine has no unchanged-output result at all. procrule reconciles both before choosing a marker, so a divergence in an -A stream is a real divergence rather than an artefact of which engine ran. Verified by running the same rules through both: identical marker streams.
.\"
.\" THE -L EXAMPLE WAS WRONG. It showed l on ISTANBUL giving istanbul and dotless-i stanbul, with -L tr selecting the latter. Under -8 that word returns istanbul for EVERY locale, because each word is routed by its own content and an all-ASCII word is run by the byte engine, which has no locale. -L was working exactly as specified; the example simply could not demonstrate it. The -8 entry now states the dispatch rule, since a reader testing the option on an ASCII wordlist would otherwise conclude it does nothing. Measured on a word that does reach the engine: istanbul-e-acute gives both variants by default, dotted-I under -L tr, plain I under -L c.
.\"
.\" TH 2026-09-15, version 1.30 to 1.34 which is where procrule.c sits. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.10  2026/09/11 15:34:42  dlr
.\" -8 is the option that runs the UTF-32 engine, and -A is documented for the first time. The flag is -8 in both procrule and mdxfind: mdxfind's -u loads a userid file, so -8 is the only spelling that can mean the same thing in both tools, and every occurrence on this page now names it. -A was implemented and had no entry anywhere: it emits one line per (word, rule) with nothing suppressed, substituting :EMPTY, :REJECT, :SAME, :BADRULE or :NEG for the line that would otherwise be absent, and forcing hex encoding on so a marker cannot be confused with a candidate. It is the counterpart to the DESCRIPTION statement that procrule never emits the source word -- the switch that makes that suppression visible and countable. The :SAME caveat is stated because it does not mean the same thing in both engines: the byte engine reports output-equals-input as its own outcome while the UTF-32 engine emits the unchanged word as an ordinary line, so the two are not comparable without normalising. SYNOPSIS gains the -A form. TH 2026-09-15 and version 1.30 to 1.34, which is where procrule.c sits.
.\" Correct the -u sentence in CHARACTER CLASSES. It said classes are not uniformly refused under -u and pointed at rules32(7); the UTF-32 engine now refuses every class form at load time, so the sentence said the opposite of what happens. It now states the refusal, that a tilde prefix is refused whatever follows it, and the part that matters to anyone with a rule file to fix: the ?? escape means the same in both engines, so correcting @? to @?? runs identically either way. No other change; the rest of the section was verified still correct against the rebuilt engine.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.9  2026/09/11 15:21:41  dlr
.\" Document the character-class syntax, which the rule engine now implements. New CHARACTER CLASSES section covering both syntaxes and what each one selects: unprefixed ?C is John table with the complement by case-toggling the letter, so ?D is not-a-digit, while ~?C is hashcat six-class table with no complement. The nine verbs that take one are listed in both forms. Three points are recorded as decisions rather than left to be inferred: ?s is hashcat class_sym() in BOTH tables and so is wider than John narrower ?s, John user classes ?0 to ?9 are not implemented and John itself refuses them undefined, and hex classes exist only in the hashcat table. That last one is what forces the two tables apart, since unprefixed an uppercase letter means not-the-lowercase-class, so a John-table ?h would make ?H mean not-lower-hex instead of hashcat uppercase-hex. Unprefixed ?h and ?H therefore name no class and are refused, which is checked and is also what John does, so the page says that rather than claiming a complement meaning the engine does not have. e?C and ~e?C are documented as different algorithms, not one algorithm with the class test swapped: John treats a class member as a separator and leaves it alone, hashcat lowercases every position then uppercases position 0 and every position whose predecessor was in the class judged on the original bytes, and e?u on aBcDe gives ABCDE against AbCdE. The operational item is the ?? escape, which is the literal-question-mark form in both syntaxes and changes what an existing rule file means: @? must now be written @?? and s?! must become s??!, and eight lines across the rule files shipped with mdxfind stop loading for exactly that reason, four in HashMob.100k.rule, three in d3ad0ne.rule and one in generated.rule. Verified by loading each file and separating the class-caused refusals from the pre-existing position ones, and verified that John rejects all eight with the same complaint, so no coverage is lost by fixing them. The failure is loud and the transcript is shown. Also noted that classes are absent under -u and not uniformly refused there, pointing at rules32(7) rather than repeating it. RULE VALIDATION now lists a class letter naming no class among the things that are diagnosed. TH date only; version stays 1.30, which is what procrule -V reports, since the change is in ruleproc.c and procrule.c is unchanged. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.8  2026/09/11 14:08:24  dlr
.\" Correct the rule documentation against ruleproc.c and record procrule behaviour that had no home. RULE VALIDATION said a malformed rule is reported with its file and line number. procrule reports neither: it prints the rule text, as "Invalid rule line. Ignored.: T!", while mdxfind is the tool that names the file and the line. The section now shows the real transcript and says which tool reports what. It also records the quieter half, which was undocumented anywhere: only a malformed or missing operand and a rule truncated mid-verb are diagnosed at all. A byte naming no verb is silently ignored and the rest of the rule still runs, so W$Z on password gives passwordZ. A hashcat rule file using verbs this engine lacks is therefore not rejected, it runs with them elided, which is a different attack and looks like a clean zero. The -u refusal list was incomplete and is now correct: control-B, the new B add-byte-value verb, and a backslash escape resolving to 0x80 or above; escapes below 0x80 are now implemented in the UTF-32 engine and agree with the byte engine, so "any use of a backslash escape" was wrong. DESCRIPTION gains the fact the page never stated: procrule never emits the source word. A rule whose output equals its input produces no line, so the no-op rule : prints nothing and a file of only : prints nothing. That is not a switchable filter, it is how the engine reports unchanged, and mdxfind depends on it. It also explains why a procrule count is below the rules-times-words product and why piping procrule into mdxfind needs the original wordlist alongside to match -r coverage. TH date 2026-09-11 and version 1.25 to 1.30, which is where procrule.c actually sits; the 1.6 check-in documented the 1.29 changes and left the TH behind. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.7  2026/09/06 14:39:41  dlr
.\" Document that -Z is standalone: it needs neither -G nor a wordlist, honours -C and -u, and splits listing (stdout) from summary (stderr).
.\"
.\" Revision 1.6  2026/09/06 14:09:41  dlr
.\" Document the 1.29 discovery changes: -l now written in -G mode, -s leads with the hit count and flags non-ascii rules, -H applies to both phases, and the new -C, -Z and -q options. Drop -r from the -G synopsis, since discovery never consulted it.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.5  2026/09/05 02:09:45  dlr
.\" Document that discovery honours -u. Without it the search runs over bytes and can report no rule where one exists on non-ASCII input.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.4  2026/09/05 01:36:49  dlr
.\" Document the UTF-32 rule engine: -u and -L, and point at the new rules32(7). -u runs the whole pipeline in UTF-32 instead of over bytes, which changes what a position means, what an operand may be and how case works; the option entry states the contract and the divergences that fit in a sentence, and the semantics live in rules32(7) rather than swelling this page. -L pins the one locale-ambiguous case mapping, the dotted and dotless I; it implies -u, it only ever narrows, and the entry carries the measured cost of the exhaustive default -- 38.7% more output on 50,000 dictionary words under four case rules, of which 27.9% is a locale the operator already knows is wrong. Neither option appears in the built-in usage. RULE VALIDATION notes that -u validates with the UTF-32 compiler, which accepts operands wider than a byte and quoted strings and refuses the undocumented control-B base64 rule and backslash escapes. SYNOPSIS gains the -u form; TH version 1.23 to 1.25. Clean under groff -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
