.\" Update for the -B fall-through fix in procrule.c 1.23. Removes the BUGS section entirely, since the -B entry was its only content and an empty heading is worse than none. Drops the See BUGS cross-reference and the do-not-combine--B-with--s workaround from the -B option, both now stale. -B gains the accurate current behaviour: counts of zero or less are rejected, and -B composes with -s. Keeps one paragraph of history under -B, because a reader on a pre-1.23 build still gets a zero-byte file named after the count and would otherwise have no explanation for it. TH version bumped 1.22 to 1.23. Renders warning-free under groff -man -ww.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: procrule(1) man page. Generation, match and discovery modes; all 17 options including -B, -M, -p and -V which the built-in usage omits. Records the -B fallthrough into the -s handler as a BUGS entry.
.\"
.TH PROCRULE 1 "2026-09-15" "procrule 1.34" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
procrule \- apply and discover password mangling rules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B procrule
.RB [ \-r
.IR rulefile ]...
.RI [ options ]
.I wordlist
.br
.B procrule
.B \-8
.RB [ \-L
.IR locale ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR rulefile ]...
.RI [ options ]
.I wordlist
.br
.B procrule
.B \-A
.RB [ \-r
.IR rulefile ]...
.RI [ options ]
.I wordlist
.br
.B procrule
.BI \-G " targetfile"
.RI [ options ]
.I wordlist
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B procrule
applies mangling rules to a wordlist.  It has two modes.
.PP
In
.I generation
mode, given only rules and a wordlist, it writes every candidate the rules
produce to standard output.  This is the same rule engine
.BR mdxfind (1)
uses through its
.B \-r
and
.B \-R
options, exposed on its own so that rule sets can be tested, filtered, or
piped into another tool.
.PP
.B procrule
never emits the source word.  A rule whose output is bytewise identical to
its input produces no line at all, so the no\-op rule
.B :
produces no output and a rule file of nothing but
.B :
prints nothing.  This is not a filter that can be switched off \(em it is how
the engine reports \(lqunchanged\(rq, and
.BR mdxfind (1)
depends on it, hashing the unmodified word once from its search loop rather
than from a rule.  It is also why a
.B procrule
candidate count is lower than the
.I "rules \(mu words"
product, and why feeding
.B procrule
output to
.BR mdxfind (1)
needs the original wordlist passed alongside it to match
.BR \-r 's
coverage.
.PP
In
.I match
mode, given one or more
.B \-m
files of known result words,
.B procrule
reports only the candidates that appear in those files, together with the
word and rule that produced them.  This is how you find out which rules in a
large set are actually earning their keep.
.PP
In
.I discovery
mode
.RB ( \-G ),
.B procrule
works backwards: given base words and target words, it searches for rules \(em
including chains of rules up to
.B \-D
deep \(em that transform the former into the latter.
.PP
Rules are interpreted over bytes by default.
.B \-8
switches the whole pipeline to UTF\-32: input is decoded from UTF\-8 to
codepoints, the rule is interpreted against codepoints, and the result is
encoded back.  That changes what a position means, what an operand may be,
and how case works, so it has a page of its own \(em see
.BR rules32 (7).
.PP
Exactly one wordlist may be given.  Any number of rule files may be given.
The names
.B stdin
and
.B stdout
may be used in place of any filename.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
Read rules from
.IR file .
May be repeated; rule sets accumulate.
.TP
.BI \-m " file"
Read result words to match against.  May be repeated.  Lines containing
control characters or a colon are stored in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
form.  With no
.B \-m
file,
.B procrule
generates all candidates instead of matching.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write output to
.I file
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-l " file"
Write matched lines to
.I file
as
.IB word : rule : result\fR,
with
.I word
.B $HEX[]
encoded when it contains a colon or a control character.  This is the
per-hit audit trail; the plain output stream carries only the resulting
words.  It is written in discovery mode too, where
.I rule
is the discovered rule or chain.  Without it a discovered rule cannot be
checked: truncate and extract rules reach short targets from almost any
long base word, and nothing in a bare rule list distinguishes that from a
real generator.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Write per-rule match statistics to
.IR file .
Each line is the hit count, a tab, and the rule:
.RS
.PP
.nf
200	u
2	c	non-ascii
.fi
.PP
A third field,
.BR non-ascii ,
is present when at least one of that
rule's matches came from a base word containing a byte at or above 0x80 \(em
a cheap answer to whether
.B \-8
mattered for that rule.  Rules are
sorted by hit count, most valuable first.  The names
.B stdout
and
.B stderr
are accepted.
.PP
The plain output stream
.RB ( \-o )
deliberately stays a bare rule
list so it can be fed straight back to
.B procrule
or hashcat.
.RE
.TP
.B \-x
Disable
.B $HEX[]
encoding on output.
.TP
.B \-A
Emit every result, one line per (word, rule) pair, with nothing suppressed.
Ordinarily a rule whose output equals its input prints nothing, so the count of
lines out is below the rules-times-words product and there is no way to tell a
rejection from an unchanged word from an empty result.
.B \-A
prints a marker instead of the missing line:
.BR :EMPTY ,
.BR :REJECT ,
.BR :SAME ,
.B :BADRULE
or
.BR :NEG .
Hex encoding is forced on, because a candidate containing a colon is
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
wrapped and so no ordinary output line can begin with one \(em that is what
keeps the markers unambiguous.  Match mode is unaffected.
.PP
Both engines report the same marker for the same outcome, deliberately, and
that is what makes
.B \-A
worth having: the byte and UTF\-32 streams can be diffed line for line.  The
two engines number their internal results inversely \(em a rejection is
.B \-1
to one and
.B \-3
to the other \(em and the UTF\-32 engine has no unchanged\-output result at
all, returning the word.  Both are reconciled before a marker is chosen, so a
divergence in the output is a real divergence and not an artefact of which
engine ran.
.PP
A rule refused when the file is read never runs, so it produces no line at all
rather than a marker.
.TP
.B \-8
Interpret rules in UTF\-32 rather than over bytes.  Input is assumed
UTF\-8; a line that is not well\-formed UTF\-8 is dropped without a
diagnostic.  Positions and lengths count grapheme clusters, an operand is a
codepoint or a quoted string, arithmetic and shift verbs are 32\-bit, and
case mapping covers 23 script blocks instead of ASCII.  Off by default, so
an existing invocation keeps the byte engine and its exact output.  See
.BR rules32 (7)
for the full semantics and for the deliberate divergences.
.PP
.B \-u
is accepted and does exactly the same thing.  It was this option's own
spelling through
.B procrule
1.30 and is kept so that a script written against that binary still runs,
but
.B \-8
is the spelling to use:
.BR mdxfind (1)
spells it
.B \-8
too, because its own
.B \-u
loads a userid file.  One letter then means one thing in both tools.
.PP
Each word is routed by its own content.  A word that is entirely ASCII is run
by the byte engine even under
.BR \-8 ,
because the two engines agree on such a word and the byte engine is faster;
UTF\-32 semantics, including
.BR \-L ,
therefore cannot change the result for it.  This is worth knowing before
testing the option: on a pure\-ASCII wordlist
.B \-8
looks like it does nothing, which is correct rather than broken.  Use a word
carrying a non\-ASCII character to see the engine work.
.TP
.BI \-L " locale"
Pin the locale for the one case mapping that has more than one right
answer, the dotted and dotless I.
.B tr
and
.B az
(also
.B turkish
and
.BR azeri )
select the Turkish and Azeri mapping;
.B c
and
.B root
(also
.B posix
and
.BR none )
select the mapping every other locale uses.  Values are matched without
regard to case, and an unrecognised value is a usage error.
.IP
With no
.BR \-L ,
both variants are emitted for any word that reaches an ambiguous mapping,
which cannot miss a candidate but is wasteful \(em on 50,000 dictionary
words under four case rules it inflates the output by 38.7%, of which 27.9%
is a locale the operator already knows is wrong.
.B \-L
only ever narrows, so a wrong declaration costs coverage rather than
producing wrong candidates.
.IP
.B \-L
implies
.BR \-8 ,
because a locale means nothing to the byte engine and silently doing
nothing looks exactly like an honest result.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose.  Repeat for more detail.
.TP
.BI \-t " count"
Worker thread count.  Valid range 1 to 32768.
.TP
.BI \-M " size"
Cache memory, with an optional
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
suffix.  Values below 64 KB draw a warning.  The allocation is attempted
immediately so that an impossible request fails at once rather than
mid-run.
.TP
.BI \-p " prime"
Force the hash table size.
.TP
.BI \-B " count"
Benchmark rule application over
.I count
iterations.  A count of zero or less is rejected.  May be combined with
.BR \-s .
.IP
On builds before procrule.c 1.23,
.B \-B
fell through into the
.B \-s
handler: the count was taken as a statistics filename, so a
.B "\-B 5"
left a zero-byte file named
.B 5
in the working directory, and the two options could not be used together at
all.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-h, \-?
Print the usage summary and exit 1.
.SS "Discovery mode"
.TP
.BI \-G " file"
Enable discovery mode and read the target words from
.IR file .
The targets are loaded exactly as
.B \-m
loads them.
Discovery honours
.BR \-8 .
Without it the search runs over bytes and can only find rules the byte
engine can express, which on non-ASCII input means it may report no rule
where one exists: a word and its grapheme-correct reversal in vocalised
Arabic yields nothing, because reversing bytes mangles the text.  With
.B \-8
the same case is explained by
.BR r ,
and by every other rule that reorders two clusters.  A locale-ambiguous
rule counts as a hit if
.I any
of its variants reaches the target.
.TP
.BI \-D " depth"
Maximum rule-chain depth, 1 to 10.  Default 3.
.B \-D 1
restricts the search to single rules.
.TP
.BI \-N " count"
Phase 2 random chain iterations.  Default 10000000.
.TP
.BI \-S " rate"
Phase 2 word sample rate as a percentage, greater than 0 and at most 100.
Default is automatic.
.TP
.BI \-H " count"
Minimum hit count for a rule to be kept, applied to Phase 1
singles and Phase 2 chains alike, on both
.B \-o
and
.BR \-s .
Default 0.
.TP
.BI \-C " classes"
Restrict the discovery catalog to the named rule classes, given as a
comma-separated list of
.BR case ,
.BR affix ,
.BR leet ,
.BR shrink ,
.BR order
and
.BR other .
The single word
.B no-shrink
excludes
.B shrink
and keeps everything else.
.PP
.RS
Length-reducing rules
.RB ( 'N ,
.IR xNM )
reach short targets
from almost any long base word, so they dominate the result while
explaining nothing.  Excluding them is usually what is wanted.
.RE
.TP
.B \-Z
Print the built-in discovery catalog as validity, class and rule, one per
line, then exit.  Use it to confirm a rule is present before concluding the
engine failed to match it.
.RS
.PP
This needs neither
.B \-G
nor a wordlist: the question it answers concerns
the catalog, not the inputs.  It honours
.B \-C
and accepts
.BR \-8 ,
though that changes nothing for this dump: the two compilers agree on every
rule in the built-in catalog, 17,320 lines identical either way.  They do
accept different sets of rules in a file you supply \(em an operand wider
than a byte is refused by one and taken by the other \(em which is what
.B \-8
is for, and is visible in the load diagnostics rather than here.  The listing goes to standard output and the
one-line summary to standard error, so the dump can be redirected cleanly.
.PP
.nf
$ procrule \-Z | awk \-F'\et' '$2 == "shrink"'
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: suppress progress meters.  Summary lines are still printed.
Progress is in any case suppressed automatically when standard error is not
a terminal, since the carriage-return meter becomes one very long line in a
log file and buries the summaries.
.SH "CHARACTER CLASSES"
Nine verbs take a character class in place of a literal operand.  The prefix
selects the class table and, with it, which reference
.B procrule
is matching:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fB?\fIC\fR      John's table:   ?l ?u ?d ?s ?v ?c ?w ?p ?a ?x ?o ?b ?y ?z
\fB~?\fIC\fR     hashcat's:      ?l ?u ?d ?s ?h ?H
.fi
.RE
.PP
In John's table an uppercase class letter is the complement of the lowercase
one, so
.B ?D
is \(lqnot a digit\(rq and
.B @?D
on
.B pass1
leaves
.BR 1 .
hashcat has no complement form.  The nine verbs are
.BI s? CY\fR,
.BI @? C\fR,
.BI e? C\fR,
.BI !? C\fR,
.BI /? C\fR,
.BI (? C\fR,
.BI )? C\fR,
.BI = N ?C
and
.BI % N ?C\fR,
each available in both syntaxes.
.PP
.B ??
is the literal\-question\-mark escape, in both syntaxes.
.B "This changes the meaning of existing rule files."
A rule that meant \(lqpurge a literal question mark\(rq must now be written
.BR @?? ,
and
.B s?!
must become
.BR s??! .
Eight lines across the rule files shipped with
.BR mdxfind (1)
stop loading for this reason \(em four in
.BR HashMob.100k.rule ,
three in
.BR d3ad0ne.rule ,
one in
.B generated.rule
\(em and John rejects all eight as well.  A rule naming no class is refused at
load time with the caret on the operand, so the failure is loud:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf '@?0\en' > bad.rule
$ procrule \-r bad.rule words.txt
  Rule: @?0
          ^
  Error: Invalid character class for command '@'
Invalid rule line. Ignored.: @?0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Three points that are decisions rather than accidents.
.B ?s
is hashcat's
.B class_sym()
in
.I both
tables \(em space plus every printable non-alphanumeric, 33 characters \(em
where John's
.B ?s
is narrower.  John's user classes
.BR ?0 \(en ?9
are not implemented, and John itself refuses them when undefined.  Hex classes
exist only in the hashcat table, which is what forces the tables apart:
unprefixed, an uppercase letter means \(lqnot the lowercase class\(rq, so a
John-table
.B ?h
would make
.B ?H
mean \(lqnot lower hex\(rq rather than hashcat's \(lquppercase hex\(rq.
Unprefixed
.B ?h
and
.B ?H
therefore name no class and are refused, exactly as in John.
.PP
.BI e? C
and
.BI ~e? C
are different algorithms, not one algorithm with the class test swapped.  John's
treats a class member as a separator and leaves it as it stands; hashcat's
lowercases every position, then uppercases position 0 and every position whose
.I predecessor
was in the class, judged on the original bytes.
.B e?u
on
.B aBcDe
gives
.BR ABCDE ;
.B ~e?u
gives
.BR AbCdE .
.PP
Classes are not implemented under
.BR \-8 ,
but a class rule cannot mean two different things there: every class form is
refused at load time, naming the rule, and a
.B ~
prefix is refused whatever follows it.  The
.B ??
escape is the exception and means the same in both engines, so a rule file
corrected from
.B @?
to
.B @??
runs identically either way.  See
.BR rules32 (7).
.SH "RULE VALIDATION"
Rules are validated as they are read: each is compiled and then test\-applied
to the probe word
.RB \(lq Password \(rq.
A malformed rule is reported and dropped rather than aborting the run.
.B procrule
names the offending
.I rule text
\(em not its file and line number, which is what
.BR mdxfind (1)
reports:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf 'T!\en' > bad.rule
$ procrule \-r bad.rule words.txt
Invalid position ! for T
Invalid rule line. Ignored.: T!
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only a malformed or missing operand, a class letter that names no class, or a
rule truncated mid\-verb, is diagnosed.  A byte that names no verb at all is
.I silently ignored,
and the rest of the rule still runs \(em
.B W$Z
on
.B password
gives
.BR passwordZ .
A hashcat rule file containing verbs this engine does not implement is
therefore not rejected; it runs with those verbs elided, which is not the
same attack.  Compare the candidate stream against the source rule file
before trusting a zero result from an unfamiliar one.
.PP
Under
.B \-8
rules are validated by the UTF\-32 compiler instead, which accepts rules the
byte compiler cannot express \(em an operand wider than one byte, or a
quoted string \(em and refuses a few the byte compiler accepts: the
undocumented control\-B base64 rule, the
.B B
add\-byte\-value verb, and a backslash escape resolving to 0x80 or above.
See
.BR rules32 (7).
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage error, an unreadable file, or an
allocation failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
Expand a wordlist through a rule set:
.PP
.RS
.nf
procrule \-r best64.rule words.txt > candidates.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find which rules actually produce known-cracked passwords, and record the
word and rule for each hit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
procrule \-r huge.rule \-m cracked.txt \-l hits.txt \e
         \-s rulestats.txt words.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Discover the rules that turn base words into observed passwords, allowing
chains of two:
.PP
.RS
.nf
procrule \-G observed.txt \-D 2 \-r seed.rule base.txt
.fi
.RE
